1. Reconnaissance

Information gathering before any intrusive testing. Better recon means better targeting later.

1.1 Passive Recon

You never send packets to the target’s own systems. You use public sources, so detection risk is almost zero.

Whois

Looks up domain registration data from registrars and registries.
Shows: registrar, creation and expiry dates, name servers, registrant organization and contacts (often hidden by privacy protection), and status flags.
Use: find who owns a domain, discover related domains, and learn the DNS provider.
Command: whois example.com. It also works on IP addresses: whois 8.8.8.8 shows the owning network and ISP.

Nslookup (DNS lookup)
DNS turns names into IPs. Querying it reveals infrastructure.

Record	Meaning
A / AAAA	IPv4 / IPv6 address
MX	Mail servers
NS	Authoritative name servers
TXT	SPF, DKIM, domain verification tokens (reveals email and SaaS services used)
CNAME	Alias to another name
PTR	Reverse lookup (IP to name)
SOA	Zone authority info
nslookup example.com
nslookup -type=MX example.com
nslookup -type=NS example.com
nslookup -type=TXT example.com
dig and host do the same with more detail.
Zone transfer (AXFR): a misconfigured DNS server may hand over its entire zone: dig axfr example.com @ns1.example.com. This is a classic misconfiguration finding.
Technically you query a DNS server, not the target, so it counts as passive.

Google Dorking
Using search operators to find information the target exposed by accident.

Operator	Use
site:	Restrict to a domain
filetype:	Specific file type (pdf, xls, log, sql)
inurl:	Text in URL (admin, login)
intitle:	Text in page title (intitle:"index of" finds open directories)
intext:	Text in body
-	Exclude a term
" "	Exact phrase

Examples:

site:example.com filetype:pdf
site:example.com inurl:admin
intitle:"index of" "backup"

The Google Hacking Database (GHDB) on Exploit-DB lists ready-made dorks. Defense: use robots.txt carefully, remove sensitive files, and use authentication.

Shodan

A search engine that scans the whole internet and indexes devices and their service banners.
You query Shodan’s database, so you don’t touch the target.
Finds: open ports, software versions, exposed webcams, databases, routers, industrial systems, and known vulnerabilities.
Filters: hostname:, org:, port:, country:, product:, os:, net: (e.g. port:3389 country:IN).
Similar tools: Censys, ZoomEye, FOFA.


1.2 Active Recon

You send traffic to the target. It can be logged or trigger IDS/IPS alerts, so written authorization is mandatory.

Ping Sweep

Sends ICMP echo requests to a whole range to find live hosts.
nmap -sn 192.168.56.0/24 (-sn means no port scan). Alternative: fping -a -g 192.168.56.0/24.
On a local network Nmap uses ARP, which is more reliable than ICMP. On remote networks it also sends TCP probes to ports 443 and 80.
Limitation: firewalls often block ICMP, so a silent host may still be alive. -Pn skips host discovery and treats all hosts as up.

Banner Grabbing

Connecting to an open port and reading the greeting the service sends, which often reveals software and version (e.g. Apache/2.2.8, vsftpd 2.3.4).
Commands:
  nc -v 192.168.56.101 21
  curl -I http://192.168.56.101
  nmap -sV 192.168.56.101
  nmap --script banner 192.168.56.101
Use: match versions to known CVEs.
Defense: hide or minimize banners (e.g. Apache ServerTokens Prod). Banners can be faked, so verify.